so I had the pleasure of visiting people  from neo core games the other week these  are the folks that make the incredible  adventures of Van Helsing they were kind  enough to show me the incredible  adventures of Van Helsing 3 which of  course I will now just refer to as van  Helsing 3 of course it's an action RPG  in the style of Diablo but one of the  things I think that has made the series  standout at least a little bit is  storytelling and not just from a  straightforward standpoint but from an  environmental standpoint van Helsing 3  of course takes place in sort of a myth  mythological Eastern European setting if  you will very dark very gothic what was  interesting of course is the the focus  on storytelling in van Helsing 3 then  you might be used to in something like  say path of Exile or something in Diablo  3 so neo course showed me three  different maps that are going to be in  van Helsing 3 as far as I could tell  they were the beginning of the game  followed by the the two sequences that  come after and one thing that struck me  was such a small thing but I think a  meaningful thing is just the way they  used the camera during cutscenes  typically in an action RPG you might see  the camera zoom in and you might see  characters speak to each other and then  it will zoom out in this case in van  Helsing 3 you saw the camera sway a  little bit from further behind and what  that did and again it seems like such a  small thing but it made me interested in  what was going on it didn't seem like  they were just trying to get the story  out of the way to move on to the action  it felt like you know this is something  that's worth paying attention to and of  course it is worth paying attention to  in this case because you have your  ghostly companion as well as a van  Helsing himself however in this case  there's there's something more to Van  Helsing 3 in the past there were three  character classes that you could choose  from in the third and final game you get  to choose from six classes the class  that I got to see was the elementalist  and the elementalist as you can imagine  is all about elemental magic with an  emphasis on fire and that came through  as I watched neo core play the game in  front of me for example one of the  biggest spells that I saw time and time  again  was a wall of fire and you don't just  cast the wall of fire but you draw it on  to the screen and that was sort of a  nice touch that enabled you to determine  the trajectory and how you were going to  damage enemies and one of the upgrades  for that spell that I saw later were the  fire snakes that would emerge from that  wall of fire and start spewing orbs of  fire at nearby enemies  I saw three levels in total the first  was a dungeon area and it was in this  case a place that you needed to be so  that you could break out a character  from the previous games out of his cell  and afterwards we then went to the hub  area and the hub area works a lot like  it did in van Helsing - van helsing -  had a tower defense minigame that you  could participate in there it's not  required but it's something that has  returned in van Helsing 3 however I  didn't get a chance to look at it at all  so I can't say whether or not the tower  defense aspect has improved in any way  or has had anything meaningful added to  it so the second area I saw was a forest  and it was a traditional action RPG kind  of place where you got some some  monsters that you might have seen before  such as centaurs and the like so the  third area was the most interesting it  was the most otherworldly sort of a  parallel dimension that you have to move  through in order to get to the next part  of the forest and in this case it also  gave me a chance to see some of the more  unusual enemies and creatures my  favorite of which is one that actually  the elementalists spawns for combat  called neter hoons I'm sure I'm  mispronouncing that but in any case  nether hounds and of course they were  helpful in addition to the snakes that  rose from the flames and spewed fire  outwards and ultimately after all of  this my takeaway was hey  then Helsing looks like another fun  action RPG in a series that has already  given us two fun action RPGs so yes I'm  looking forward to playing the  incredible adventures of Van Helsing on  the surface it looks pretty much like a  standard action RPG but I think when it  comes to action RPGs it really comes  down to the  details and in this case it seems like  the story details some of the visual  details and the world details are going  to be enough to keep me interested so  stay tuned a game spot I'll give you  more information as it comes  you  [Music]
 

